<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nueces River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nueces River is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> that flows south-east into Nueces Bay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> Bay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Under Spanish rule it marked the approximate northern limit of the province of Nuevo Santander. After Texan independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> treated the Nueces as the boundary of Texas, and the disputed land between it and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> became a cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> in 1846.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nueces-river.jpg" alt="Nueces River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Billy Hathorn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nueces_River_between_La_Pryor_and_Uvalde,_TX_IMG_4256.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
